# Target C-II — near-unit pentagon diagonals are a linear-rank train-rate sensor system

**Date:** 2026-09-15
**Status:** proof-only exact differential strengthening
**Scope:** any vertex-disjoint family of bounded convex unit pentagons with one selected hidden diagonal per face
**Dependencies:** audited train-rate parametrization; bounded-face convexity; no-bounded-return train theorem; high-`t` residual/near-contact matching for the asymptotic corollary
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_hidden_diagonal_train_sensor.py`
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The residual theorem produces a linear matching of hidden pentagon diagonals of length `1+o(1)`. This note identifies the exact first-order quantity that those near contacts measure.

Let a bounded convex equilateral pentagon contain consecutive boundary vertices

\[
x,z,y
\]

and orient the two unit side vectors along the face as

\[
a=z-x,\qquad b=y-z.
\]

Let `tau_a,tau_b` be the complete stopped trains containing those two boundary occurrences. They are distinct: if one complete train had both terminal occurrences on the same bounded face, it would violate the audited no-bounded-return theorem.

The hidden diagonal is

\[
d=y-x=a+b.
\]

For a contact infinitesimal flex with train angular rates `omega_tau`, the audited train parametrization gives

\[
\dot a=\omega_{\tau_a}Ja,
\qquad
\dot b=\omega_{\tau_b}Jb.
\]

Therefore

\[
\begin{aligned}
{d\over dt}|d|^2
&=2(a+b)\cdot
\bigl(\omega_{\tau_a}Ja+\omega_{\tau_b}Jb\bigr)\\
&=2\det(a,b)
\bigl(\omega_{\tau_a}-\omega_{\tau_b}\bigr).
\end{aligned}
\tag{HS.1}
\]

The sign assumes the standard counterclockwise face orientation; reversing all orientations reverses both determinant conventions but not the content.

## 1. Near-unit diagonals have a nonvanishing sensor coefficient

Because `|a|=|b|=1`,

\[
a\cdot b={|d|^2-2\over2}.
\]

Strict convexity gives `det(a,b)>0`, and hence

\[
2\det(a,b)
=2\sqrt{1-\left({|d|^2-2\over2}\right)^2}.
\tag{HS.2}
\]

If `|d|\to1`, then

\[
\boxed{2\det(a,b)\to\sqrt3.}
\tag{HS.3}
\]

Thus the hidden diagonal is not becoming first-order insensitive as it approaches activation. Its squared-length derivative converges to `sqrt(3)` times one adjacent train-rate difference. For example `|d|<=1.01` already gives coefficient greater than `1.70`.

In particular, under any contact flex,

\[
{d\over dt}|d|^2=o(1)
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\omega_{\tau_a}-\omega_{\tau_b}=o(1)
\tag{HS.4}
\]

uniformly on a family whose selected diagonals converge to one.

## 2. The sensor multigraph has maximum degree two

Now take a vertex-disjoint family `\mathcal G` of pentagons and choose one hidden diagonal in each. For each selected diagonal use the shorter two-edge boundary path and form a multigraph `H_{sens}`:

- vertices are complete stopped trains;
- the edge corresponding to pentagon `P` joins the two trains on the two boundary sides spanning its chosen diagonal.

There are no loops by no-bounded-return.

A complete stopped train has exactly two terminal occurrences. A sensor incidence at a train uses one terminal side occurrence on its selected pentagon. Since the pentagons are vertex-disjoint, the same terminal occurrence cannot serve two selected faces. Hence

\[
\boxed{\Delta(H_{sens})\le2.}
\tag{HS.5}
\]

Parallel edges are possible only if the same two trains terminate together at both ends; this causes no problem below.

Therefore every nontrivial connected component of `H_sens` is a path or a cycle, with a two-edge parallel cycle allowed.

## 3. Linear rank of hidden-contact derivative rows

Let `B_sens` be the oriented incidence matrix of `H_sens`, one row per selected pentagon and one column per train. Equation (HS.1) says that the vector of hidden-diagonal squared-length derivatives is

\[
D_{hid}\omega
=
\operatorname{diag}(c_P)B_{sens}\omega,
\qquad
c_P=2\det(a_P,b_P)>0.
\tag{HS.6}
\]

Thus diagonal scaling does not change rank:

\[
\operatorname{rank}D_{hid}
=\operatorname{rank}B_{sens}.
\]

A path component with `e` edges has incidence rank `e`. A cycle component with `e>=2` edges has rank `e-1>=e/2`. Summing components gives

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{rank}D_{hid}
\ge {|\mathcal G|\over2}.
}
\tag{HS.7}

This is a finite exact statement. No genericity, stress sign, or phase separation is used.

## 4. High-`t` residual corollary

In the `R_F=o(D)` branch of the high-`t` residual theorem, there is a vertex-disjoint pentagon family `\mathcal G_D` with

\[
|\mathcal G_D|
\ge\left({p_5(T)\over21}-o(1)\right)D
\]

and one selected hidden diagonal per face satisfying `|d|-1=o(1)` uniformly. Applying (HS.3) and (HS.7), the source carries a train-rate sensor operator with

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{rank}D_{hid}
\ge\left({p_5(T)\over42}-o(1)\right)D,
}
\tag{HS.8}

whose nonzero row coefficients converge uniformly to `sqrt(3)`.

Representative safe densities are therefore one half of the certified hidden-matching densities: about `0.0084D` at `T=1.88`, `0.0072D` at `1.90`, `0.0060D` at `1.92`, `0.00365D` at `1.96`, and `0.00125D` at `2.00`.

## 5. What this does and does not solve

The residual matching is therefore not merely a geometric population of near contacts. It is a linear-rank family of explicit train-rate difference functionals. Any contact flex that keeps all selected hidden diagonals first-order stationary must equalize train angular rates along every component of `H_sens`.

This does **not** yet produce a diameter-feasible flex. The canonical positive KKT stress and mixed rigidity still block nontrivial first-order motions that preserve all old contacts while making every active diameter nonincreasing. Nor does (HS.7) by itself prove that these sensor rows remain independent after restriction to `ker C_face`.

The next exact question is now algebraic and global:

```text
face-closure kernel ker C_face
+ max-degree-2 sensor graph H_sens
+ diameter-thrackle derivative map
-> can all p5(T)D/42 hidden-contact sensor directions be blocked
   without forcing linear R_F or an actual unit diagonal?
```

That is strictly narrower than generic simultaneous activation.

**Disposition:** proved exact hidden-diagonal/train-rate derivative identity and linear sensor-rank corollary in the sublinear-residual branch. C-II and Level I remain open.
